  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Saturday: Hot conditions are expected to continue\nthrough at least Friday under the influence of the southwestern\nridge of high pressure. High temperatures through Friday will be\nin the 90s to lower 100s, leading to Moderate to Major HeatRisk. This\nlevel of heat will be of risk to much of the population, \nespecially anyone without effective cooling or hydration. Air \nquality alerts continue through Friday morning for much of \ncentral and eastern Washington from smoke from local and \nregional fires. When air quality is Unhealthy, everyone should \nreduce exposure. Limit time outside and avoid strenuous \nactivity.\n\nElevated to critical fire weather conditions are expected\nstarting tonight and lasting through at least Saturday. An upper\nlevel low off the California coast continues to slowly meander\nnorth today, bringing increased PWATs around 180-200% of normal\nto the Inland Northwest. This will increase concerns for \nlightning starting tonight in the Cascades. Synoptic lift is not\nparticularly impressive but ongoing thunderstorm activity in \nwestern Washington and northern Oregon tomorrow afternoon will \nmove towards the Cascades. Elevated instability will be decent \naround 200-600 J/kg, sufficient for lightning strikes into \nThursday morning. As such, we have issued Red Flag Warnings for \nmuch of the Cascades, Western Okanogan county, and the Okanogan \nHighlands starting at 2 AM tonight and lasting through 2 AM \nFriday. The Colville Reservation also has a red flag warning, \nbut the morning thunderstorm activity looks to be mainly west of\nthe area so it does not start until 1 PM Thursday. \nThunderstorms will have some precipitation falling out of them \nwith higher PWATs, but strikes outside of thunderstorm cores and\ngusty outflow winds up to 35 mph raise concern for new fire \nstarts that rapidly spread. There is also a 20-25% chance of a \nstronger thunderstorm in the Blue mountains and the Camas \nPrairie tomorrow afternoon that would support gusts around 50 \nmph. With onshore flow Thursday afternoon in western Washington \nand increasing pressure gradients, Cascade gap winds around \n10-15 mph with gusts up 20 mph are possible Thursday evening. \n\nSynoptic winds will continue to trend upward Friday and especially\nSaturday as pressure gradients continue to increase. The \ngreatest chance for wind/low humidity overlap Friday will be in \nthe east slopes of the Cascades, Waterville Plateau, Okanogan \nValley, and Western Columbia basin with 10-20 mph southwest \nwinds gusting around 20-30 mph. The synoptics are impressive \nSaturday with a 100 kt upper level jet overhead and a deepening \nlow pressure in the lee of the Canadian Rockies. 700 mb winds \nwill be around 30-45 kt, which is into the 99th percentile for \nthis time of year. With that in mind, sustained surface winds \nwill be westerly/southwesterly around 15-30 mph, with gusts \naround 25-45 mph. Any new or existing wildfires Saturday will be\ncapable of very rapid fire spread. Fire weather watches have \nbeen issued for both Friday and Saturday for portions of the \nregion. \n\nSunday through Wednesday: The overall longwave pattern looks to\nremain fairly steady through next week. The desert southwest\nridge will stay anchored and longwave troughing will continue in\nthe Gulf of Alaska. This will favor elevated winds and mostly\ndry conditions to continue for the Inland Northwest.\nTemperatures will be near normal in the 80s and 90s with breezy\nconditions continuing. /DB",
